an><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">vs.</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:1em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">not all the </span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">’s are equal</span></span></span></span></span></span>","enumerator":"1","key":"pZmtuHqMCx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conceptually, the null hypothesis claims that the number of words in ads is not related to the educational level of the magazine, and the alternative hypothesis claims that there is a relationship.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"Ujf1m6QPku"}],"key":"c3DuIoGoM6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 2: Checking conditions and summarizing the data.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"W5NOnO19Sp"}],"key":"YQdBmlNQLZ"}],"key":"XJwPZpBtbG"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ads were selected at random from each magazine group, so the three samples are independent.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"JrxCbHI6cD"}],"key":"Z98usmiSR7"}],"key":"n479yhM2SD"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Side-by-side boxplots of the data do not display any alarming violations of the normality assumption. There is some skewness in groups 2 and 3, but not extremely so, and there are no outliers in the data.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"No96jqUs81"}],"key":"pkha6zxaHv"}],"key":"ZK4umXgKmC"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sample standard deviations are 74.0 (group 1), 64.3 (group 2), and 57.6 (group 3). We can assume that the equal standard deviation condition is met, since the rule of thumb is satisfied: ","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"ML0CDWTf7B"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"74.0/57.6 < 2","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>74.0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>57.6</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">74.0/57.6 &lt; 2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">74.0/57.6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>","key":"Pc2pmHIS3D"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"H0hwhvQWhj"}],"key":"DwHdAKLh5D"}],"key":"nztlz3CllA"}],"key":"EqxdqAC2SC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before we move on, let’s look again at the data. It is easy to see the trend of the sample means. However, there is so much variation within each of the groups that there is almost complete overlap between the three boxplots, and the differences between the means are overshadowed and seem like something that could have happened just by chance. Let’s move on and see whether the ","key":"BExJd8GKdt"},{"type":"abbreviation","title":"Analysis of Variance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ANOVA","key":"L2nGZvMubt"}],"key":"FielgnCjxL"},{"type":"text","value":" F-test supports this observation.","key":"nTBxXd8OUS"}],"key":"fArOGeKwP6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using statistical software to conduct the ","key":"EbrrQTylXl"},{"type":"abbreviation","title":"Analysis of Variance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ANOVA","key":"viWrua3FLv"}],"key":"o9V7V5oUvT"},{"type":"text","value":" F-test, we find that the F-statistic is 1.18, which is not very large. We also find that the p-value is 0.317.","key":"cUSDgtr5vo"}],"key":"LL4zp86bhg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 3: Finding the p-value.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"a1QJYuxtUv"}],"key":"dITFpNQPAa"},{"type":"text","value":" The p-value is 0.317, which tells us that getting data like those observed is not very surprising assuming that there are no differences between the three magazine groups with respect to the mean number of words in ads (which is what ","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"IJLgzDz3MF"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_0","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"YVrsuv8XmG"},{"type":"text","value":" claims). In other words, the large p-value tells us that it is quite reasonable that the differences between the observed sample means could have happened just by chance (i.e., due to sampling variability) and not because of true differences between the means.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"WyCwKcTpyr"}],"key":"Y53zb5JjaR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 4: Making conclusions in context.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"z7qMw8Ncyv"}],"key":"OBz5dpCrUw"},{"type":"text","value":" The large p-value indicates that the results are not significant, and that we cannot reject ","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"HCQJCHj7Ka"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_0","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"dIb1UR1BDZ"},{"type":"text","value":". We therefore conclude that the study does not provide evidence that the mean number of words in ads is related to the educational level of the magazine. In other words, the study does not provide evidence that advertisers alter the reading level of their ads (as measured by the number of words) based on the educational level of the target audience of the magazine.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"TaLPsJyvhu"}],"key":"lCWxtiu7eh"}],"class":"tip","key":"UP6riicCh3"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Within-Group Variation and Significance","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"YThhH5Kzcw"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-within-group-variation-and-significance","label":"Check Your Understanding: Within-Group Variation and Significance","html_id":"check-your-understanding-within-group-variation-and-significance","implicit":true,"key":"S2excyOAgo"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a5666538464903eb44b37d3c2138adaa.mjs","model":{"question":"In the magazine ads example, the three sample means (140.0, 121.4, 106.5) look quite different, yet the test was not significant. Which feature of the data explains this?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The variation within each group is very large compared to the differences among the sample means","feedback":"Correct! The within-group standard deviations (57-74 words) are large relative to the differences among the means (~33 words at most), so the boxplots overlap almost completely and F is small (1.18)."},{"correct":false,"text":"The sample sizes were unequal","feedback":"The samples were the same size (18 each); the issue is the within-group variability."},{"correct":false,"text":"The conditions for the test were violated","feedback":"The conditions were checked and satisfied; the non-significance reflects weak evidence, not an invalid test."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Look at the standard deviations (74.0, 64.3, 57.6) relative to the differences among the means.","explanation":""},"key":"JCn5hgJ1EZ"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a5666538464903eb44b37d3c2138adaa.mjs","model":{"question":"Suppose the same three sample means had come from samples with standard deviations of about 20 words each. How would the test likely change?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"F would be much larger and the result likely significant","feedback":"Correct! Smaller within-group variation would shrink F's denominator, making F much larger and the p-value smaller—likely a significant result."},{"correct":false,"text":"F would be smaller and the result less significant","feedback":"Reducing within-group spread makes differences among means MORE detectable, not less."},{"correct":false,"text":"Nothing would change","feedback":"The F-statistic depends on both variations, so it would definitely change."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Shrinking the denominator of F...","explanation":""},"key":"ZzPlkl4JFs"}],"key":"rWPLo20r5L"}],"key":"CFLvae3UI5"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The ANOVA F-Test: P-value and Conclusion","url":"/pages/p250-m16-anova-5-of-7","group":"Section 13: Inference for Relationships"},"next":{"title":"After ANOVA: Which Means Differ?","url":"/pages/p252-m16-anova-7-of-7","group":"Section 13: Inference for Relationships"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}